  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 310 AM EDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nHeat will remain the primary weather concern through Wednesday, with \nhigh temperatures in the lower 90s, overnight lows only cooling into \nthe mid-70s (not much nocturnal relief), and daytime dew points \nreaching the mid- to upper-70s (very humid). Apparent temperatures \nwill reach or exceed 100 degrees each day across most of our \nforecast area (locally dependent on thunderstorms and cloud cover), \nand we may approach Heat Advisory criteria Tuesday and Wednesday, \nespecially south and east of the Atlanta metro. At least isolated to \nscattered thunderstorms will also remain possible each afternoon and \nevening not just through Wednesday, but over each of the next 7 \ndays. Lightning, gusty winds, and localized flash flooding will \nremain concerns with the stronger storms each day, while \norganized/widespread severe weather is not currently expected. \nTropical Depression 2 has formed over the eastern Gulf and is \ncurrently forecast to strengthen into a tropical storm. However, \nconfidence is increasing that this system will have no direct \nimpacts locally across northern and central Georgia as it turns west \nand moves away from our area toward the northwestern Gulf region.\n\nTroughing over the eastern/northeastern CONUS will eventually \nsupport a cold front reaching down to Georgia in the Wednesday night \ninto Thursday morning timeframe, only taking 3-4 degrees off of high \ntemperatures Thursday vs. Wednesday, but noticeably reducing dew \npoints especially across the northern half of the CWA (reaching the \n60s instead of the 70s). Temperatures and dew points will hold \nfairly steady Friday, before the humidity starts to increase again \nSaturday and Sunday. Apparent temperatures will range from about 90 \nto 100 during this post-frontal period.\n\nWith a ridge of high pressure centered to our west over Texas and \nOklahoma by mid-week, Georgia will be positioned in northwest flow \ndown the east side of the ridge toward the end of the week into next \nweekend. Any troughs/disturbances riding this flow could act to \nfocus periods of convection at times. Another cold frontal passage \nappears possible between Saturday night and Sunday night, but \nconfidence is low at this range on exact timing and whether this \nwill provide much relief from our generally hot and humid summer \nairmass.",
